> tl;dr
>
> - Transformed 15-site UNDRR Drupal 10 ecosystem: 50–70% faster load times, 20% Azure cost reduction, doubled Lighthouse SEO scores.
> - Navigated Azure migration challenges including database bottlenecks that caused cold-path failures on long-tail content.
> - Diagnosed and resolved performance issues through rigorous benchmarking: moved from Azure Database for MySQL to MariaDB on VM, cutting DB latency by ~51%.
> - Recovered operational reliability: daily page failures from 6 post-migration peak to ~4.5, trending toward ~4 baseline; 503 errors down ~77%.
> - User satisfaction: 79.9% report sites helpful (+7 percentage points year over year; 35,113 responses).
> - Managed through risk-led governance with 3-week release cadence and cross-functional coordination across Geneva, Bangkok, Bonn, New York, Manila.

## The vision

We operate a multi-domain ecosystem serving policymakers, practitioners, and the public with critical disaster risk reduction information. A tidy "site redesign" isn't enough—the platform has to be fast, reliable, and readable by both humans and machines. When we kicked off this transformation, we framed success in terms stakeholders care about: measurable speed, lower infrastructure spend, clearer routes for AI-ready publishing, and demonstrable user trust.

The starting point: critical content for decision-makers was slowed by inconsistent theming, fragmented governance, and limited AI-readiness. Pages were heavy, image pipelines inconsistent, and analytics lacked dimensions for segment-level reporting. We needed comprehensive platform standardization across 15 sites while simultaneously managing a major infrastructure migration.

## The migration challenge

We migrated from a traditional VM setup with custom load balancing to Azure—a significant infrastructure change that promised better scalability and operational efficiency. But some planning decisions proved suboptimal. We chose services that looked good on paper but weren't designed with Drupal's architecture in mind, particularly Azure Database for MySQL.

The problems manifested in unexpected ways. High-traffic pages worked fine—they stayed warm in cache. But the long tail of less-frequently accessed content would fail. If no one had visited a particular page recently, the first request would time out. This pattern was nearly invisible on popular routes but created a poor experience for users exploring deeper content or accessing regional pages.

Daily page failures increased from ~4 to ~6. Users reported timeouts during peak traffic. Non-cached paths and admin UX regressed substantially. Query times and p95 latency suggested the managed database service was the bottleneck. Perceived stability declined despite strategic platform investments.

## Diagnosis: database bottleneck

We needed rigorous evidence to justify a major infrastructure change. Load testing alternated between Azure Database for MySQL and MariaDB on a VM while keeping code and configuration static to isolate the database layer. We measured both user-facing paths and CRUD micro-benchmarks, neutralizing cache effects to see the real performance delta.

The results were consistent: MariaDB on a VM outperformed the managed service by 2-3x on database-bound operations. The bottleneck was clear. The full technical analysis and benchmarking tables are documented in [Drupal delayed by Azure MySQL](/posts/20250710-metrics-of-caution-azure-mysql/).

## Recovery and optimization

With diagnosis complete, we executed a coordinated recovery across multiple fronts:

**Database migration**: Moved to MariaDB on a VM with tight backup and continuity plans. This immediately cut DB-bound latency by ~51% and reduced daily 503 errors by ~77%. Editor time-to-first-byte improved materially, especially on admin paths and heavy queries.

**Platform standardization**: Consolidated asset handling with deterministic responsive images in build. Standardized Drupal 10 theming with design-system patterns and component libraries across all 15 sites. Unified UX while maintaining site-specific requirements.

**Performance engineering**: Implemented comprehensive caching strategy covering page cache, render cache, and CDN layers. Tuned image pipelines for Core Web Vitals compliance. Conducted render path audits and fixed performance regressions. Added cache warming for cold paths to prevent the timeout pattern we saw post-migration.

**AI-readiness**: Implemented RDF/SKOS metadata pipelines and JSON-LD exports. Built machine-actionable content models positioning UNDRR as a leader in structured data publishing. Full context-first approach documented in [Smart AI integration](/posts/20250919-smart-ai-integration-context-first/).

**Analytics and monitoring**: Deployed GA4 with custom dimensions for segment-level reporting. Built Looker Studio dashboards tracking chatbot referral patterns and AI-driven traffic. Hardened monitoring with failure categorization by root cause and owner. Implemented on-site user feedback to validate that numbers matched experience.

## Governance: managing complexity

This level of transformation—platform standardization, infrastructure migration, performance recovery, and feature delivery—required disciplined governance to stay on track.

We introduced a living risk register with owners, triggers, and mitigations to steer prioritization. The database migration was flagged early as high-impact/high-risk, which allowed us to plan the transition carefully and maintain service continuity.

We committed to a predictable 3-week release cadence with scope control and demo checkpoints. This gave stakeholders clear expectations about what would ship when, and gave the team focus during a period of significant platform change.

We required measurable outcomes on every major ticket—not just "improve performance" but "reduce p95 TTFB to under 1s on admin paths." This aligned work artifacts to organizational goals and made reporting clearer.

The cadence held throughout 2024-2025. 90%+ of major tickets included defined outcomes and success metrics. The risk register was reviewed each cycle with owner sign-off. Stakeholder confidence increased as we demonstrated predictable delivery even while managing platform complexity.

## Outcomes

**Performance**:
- 50–70% faster page loads across 15 sites
- Median response time: 0.45s (improved from 0.88s on legacy platform)
- Database latency: ~51% reduction after MariaDB migration
- Doubled Lighthouse SEO scores on representative pages

**Reliability**:
- Daily page failures: recovered from post-migration peak of ~6 to ~4.5, trending toward pre-migration baseline of ~4
- 503 errors: ~77% reduction after database migration
- Stability visible to users: smoother experience on long-tail and regional content

**Cost optimization**:
- 20% quarterly Azure spend reduction
- Better resource utilization through right-sized infrastructure

**User satisfaction**:
- 79.9% of visitors report sites are helpful (+7 percentage points year over year)
- 35,113 user responses (+4.3% year over year volume)
- Perception matched reality: instrumentation validated that fixes improved actual user experience

**Campaign impact**:
- GAR2025 landing: ~40% engagement lift during campaign period (see [GAR2025 case study](/work/2025/case-study-gar2025-landing/))
- Platform stability enabled successful high-stakes launches

**AI readiness**:
- Machine-actionable metadata positioned UNDRR as industry leader
- Structured publishing workflows supporting both human and AI audiences
- Analytics tracking AI-driven referral patterns and behavior shifts

## What made this work

This wasn't a heroics story—it was the result of standardization, rigorous diagnosis, and patient execution. We focused on high-leverage improvements and applied them consistently across 15 sites. The compounding effect is what moved the needle.

Cross-functional coordination across platform, product, editorial, design, and operations teams in Geneva, Bangkok, Bonn, New York, and Manila. Clear ownership, disciplined cadence, and evidence-based decision making. When we hit the database bottleneck, we didn't guess—we benchmarked rigorously and made the infrastructure change with data in hand.

We also kept listening to users throughout. The 79.9% satisfaction score wasn't an accident—it reflected our commitment to ensuring that technical metrics translated to real improvements in user experience.

